ZLIB DATA COMPRESSION LIBRARY

zlib 1.3.2.1 is a general purpose data compression library.  All the code is
thread safe (though see the FAQ for caveats).  The data format used by the zlib
library is described by RFCs (Request for Comments) 1950 to 1952 at
https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc1950 (zlib format), rfc1951 (deflate
format) and rfc1952 (gzip format).

All functions of the compression library are documented in the file zlib.h
(volunteer to write man pages welcome, contact zlib@gzip.org).  A usage example
of the library is given in the file test/example.c which also tests that
the library is working correctly.  Another example is given in the file
test/minigzip.c.  The reference C compression library itself is composed of all
source files in the root directory; a memory-safe Rust reimplementation of the
same library, exporting the same C ABI, is in the Cargo workspace under crates/
and is described below.

To compile all files and run the test program, follow the instructions given at
the top of Makefile.in.  In short "./configure; make test", and if that goes
well, "make install" should work for most flavors of Unix.  For Windows, use
one of the special makefiles in win32/ or contrib/vstudio/ .  For VMS, use
make_vms.com.

The compression and decompression engine has also been reimplemented in Rust,
in the Cargo workspace under crates/ .  It is a reimplementation and not a
binding: crates/zlib-rs holds every algorithm under #![forbid(unsafe_code)]
with an empty dependency list, and crates/libz-rs-sys holds the raw pointers a
C ABI unavoidably has, exposing that ABI so that existing callers link against
it unchanged.  zlib.h, zconf.h and zlib.map are unmodified; the C sources in
this directory are retained and are the reference the Rust code is diffed
against, so "./configure; make test" continues to build and test the C library
exactly as before, on machines with no Rust toolchain at all.

Each crate has a distinct job.  crates/zlib-rs is the algorithmic core --
deflate, inflate, the Huffman coder, both checksums, the gzip file layer and
the one-shot wrappers.  With no features it is no_std plus alloc and the gzip
file layer is not compiled at all, since a gzFile is a real file and only the
standard library can open one.  crates/libz-rs-sys is the C ABI layer and the
only shipped crate that uses unsafe, under #![deny(unsafe_op_in_unsafe_fn)] so
that each unsafe operation is scoped on its own and carries a comment naming
the invariant behind it; it builds as a cdylib, a staticlib and an rlib under
the library name z, which is why cargo emits libz.so and libz.a rather than
Rust-flavoured names.  crates/zlib-rs-differential is a development-only
harness that compiles the C sources in this directory as a test oracle;
neither shipped crate depends on it, so an ordinary build of the Rust library
never compiles them.

Where unsafe lives is worth stating by count rather than in round numbers,
because "the only crate permitted to write unsafe" is a statement about the
SHIPPED library and is often read as a statement about the whole tree.

In the shipped library, every occurrence is under crates/libz-rs-sys/src/ --
1,153 of them across nine files when this was last counted, none anywhere else.
Re-derive the numbers rather than trusting them, and use this command rather
than the obvious one:

    grep -rn --include='*.rs' -E 'unsafe *(\{|fn|extern|impl)' crates fuzz benches \
      | grep -vE ':[0-9]+:[[:space:]]*(//|/\*|\*)'

The second stage is not decoration.  This tree documents itself heavily, and the
pattern matches PROSE as readily as code: at the time of writing the unfiltered
command reports 1,554 hits where the filtered one reports 1,527, and five of the
difference fall in crates that provably contain no unsafe at all -- a doc comment
quoting "unsafe { vsnprintf(...) }" is a match.  With the filter it counts
constructs, and the three figures given here sum to exactly what it prints.
Treat any count in a document as a snapshot; the command is the authority.

crates/zlib-rs, which is the whole of the algorithm, carries
#![forbid(unsafe_code)] and therefore has zero -- checked by the compiler on
every build rather than by grep, which is why it is worth stating separately from
any number.

Outside the shipped library, three dev-only places have unsafe, and each is
unavoidable rather than tolerated.  crates/zlib-rs-differential/src/oracle.rs
declares the C oracle and src/port.rs holds the safe boundary gates that
everything else drives the C ABI through (273 occurrences between them); and the
suites under crates/libz-rs-sys/tests/ have 101, because CALLING an extern "C"
function is itself an unsafe operation, so a test that exercises the facade's own
entry points cannot avoid an unsafe block however safe the code under test is.
Those gates are deliberately not part of the shipped facade: AAP 0.6.1 fixes that
facade's unsafe at exactly the six unavoidable C-ABI site categories, and a test
harness is not one of them.  The facade's own crate documentation states those
six categories at the width the code uses them, and every unsafe block there
carries a // SAFETY: comment naming its invariant -- which clippy enforces
through undocumented_unsafe_blocks rather than leaving to review.

What that buys is measurable, and it is compiler-enforced rather than counted:
the three benchmark suites, all five fuzz targets, the differential crate's three
test suites and its build script each carry #![forbid(unsafe_code)] of their own,
as do the safe core and every one of its test suites.  Zero there is a build
failure if it ever stops being true.

For a program that dynamically links libz, the exported symbol set is the same,
the z_stream and gz_header layouts are the same, the SONAME is still libz.so.1,
and zlib.h and zconf.h are installed unchanged, so that program keeps working
without being recompiled.  That is a measured result on one target,
x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu: it is where the staged library, the symbol diff and
the relinked C drivers are checked.  Staging the drop-in needs a linker that
accepts -soname and --version-script, so it is offered on the ELF platforms
that get zlib.map for the C shared library, and the CMake path stops with a
message on anything else -- not-UNIX, Apple, AIX and SunOS.  The STATIC library
has no such requirement and is supported wherever cargo runs.  Building and
testing the Rust workspace is a wider claim than either and is made separately,
by triple: see the note under "Notes for some targets" below.

Static linking needs one thing dynamic linking does not, and both package paths
supply it.  A Rust libz.a carries the Rust runtime's own object code, whose
undefined references reach into libc and its neighbours, and an archive cannot
record a dependency the way a shared object records DT_NEEDED; the set differs
per target and only rustc knows it.  So it is measured once and published
through the standard metadata a consumer already reads: the installed zlib.pc
gains a single Libs.private line, which is what "pkg-config --static --libs
zlib" reports, and the CMake package publishes the same set as the exported
ZLIB::ZLIBSTATIC target's interface libraries.  The historical "Libs: ... -lz"
line is untouched on every path, so a consumer that reads either channel keeps
writing the link command it always wrote.

The two build systems differ only in when that descriptor is written.  CMake
knows at configure time whether the Rust library is being built, so with
-DZLIB_BUILD_RUST=ON it installs exactly one zlib.pc and that one carries the
line.  The autotools path stages the Rust library rather than installing it, so
"make rust-pc" writes the descriptor beside the staged library, gates it by
building and running a C program from nothing but the flags pkg-config answers,
and prints the single command that installs it over the installed zlib.pc; do
that whenever you install the staged libz.a.  A consumer that reads neither
channel -- one that hardcodes "-lz" -- has to add the set itself, and every one
of these commands prints it.

The compressed bytes are the same as well, over the configurations the
differential tests compare: the encoder reproduces the reference
implementation's own heuristics rather than improving on them, and streams
interoperate in both directions -- what the C library deflates the Rust library
inflates, and the other way round.

To build and check the Rust library:

    make rust          # stage the drop-in libz under target/dropin/
    make rust-test     # link the unmodified C drivers against it and run them
    make rust-symbols  # diff the staged symbol set against a built C library

"make rust-symbols" is a comparison and not a listing.  It reports how many
symbols each library exports and which names one has that the other does not,
so it needs a C shared library built in this tree -- "./configure && make",
which builds libz.so.<ZLIB_VERSION>, or ZLIBREF=/path/to/one -- and stops with
that instruction when there is none.  The name is not spelled out here on
purpose: configure derives it from ZLIB_VERSION in zlib.h, so writing a literal
version into this file is how it comes to name a target that does not exist.
"make rust" performs the same diff as one of its own checks, but only when such
a reference happens to be there already.

"make rust" and "make rust-test" do not need ./configure at all: they compose
the shared-object link from Makefile.in's own defaults and name the staged
library from ZLIB_VERSION in zlib.h, so they work on an unconfigured tree.
What ./configure adds is the C library build that "make rust-symbols" diffs
against, and a $(CARGO) recorded for you (unconfigured, name it yourself with
"make rust CARGO=cargo").

Four more targets complete the set:

    make rust-pc       # emit the pkg-config descriptor and gate a consumer
    make rust-header   # regenerate the C header with cbindgen and gate it
    make rust-msrv     # check the two shipped crates on the 1.80 floor
    make rust-clean    # remove what the Rust targets produced

"make rust-pc" is where the one packaging difference between the two
implementations is handled.  Dynamic linking is identical -- -lz resolves to
either library and a shared object records its own dependencies -- but a Rust
static archive carries the Rust runtime and so needs that runtime's native
libraries on a static link line, while the C libz.a needs none.  One zlib.pc
cannot say both, so that target writes a second descriptor beside the staged
library, with a Libs.private line and every other field taken from zlib.pc
unchanged, and then compiles, links and runs a C program from nothing but the
flags pkg-config answers for it.  Install that file in place of zlib.pc
whenever you install the staged Rust library; the target prints the copy
command.  "make rust-test" runs it for you.  The CMake build generates and
installs the equivalent descriptor itself.

The set in that line is probed once, by "make rust", with "rustc --print
native-static-libs" for the target being built, and recorded in
target/dropin/native-static-libs.stage.  Everything that needs it reads it from
there: the relink of the shared library, the static links "make rust-test"
performs against libz.a, and the descriptor above.  One probe and one recorded
answer is the point -- a second probe is a second thing to disagree with -- and
a target whose rustc cannot be run is not stuck: RUSTPCPRIVATE states the set
instead, and every command that needs it stops with that instruction rather than
linking against a requirement set it does not know.

Ask the compiler for the set; do not assemble one by hand.  On this kind of host
it is seven libraries, -lgcc_s among them, and a plausible short list such as
"-lm -lpthread -lc" configures, builds, and then fails in a consumer's link with
an undefined reference to _Unwind_Resume.  The probe needs an input file, which
is easy to miss -- rustc with no source exits saying "no input filename given":

    : > /tmp/pc-probe.rs
    rustc --print native-static-libs --crate-type staticlib \
        [--target <triple>] -o /tmp/pc-probe.a /tmp/pc-probe.rs
    make rust RUSTPCPRIVATE="<the note: line it prints>"

The answer is target-specific, so run it for the target you are building.

"make rust-header" wants cbindgen as well as a cargo reachable on PATH, and
"make rust-clean" wants no cargo at all.  The comment at the top of Makefile.in
describes all seven targets, including what each one does and does not
establish.

CMake can point the exported ZLIB::ZLIB and ZLIB::ZLIBSTATIC targets at the
Rust library instead of the C one:

    cmake -S . -B build -DZLIB_BUILD_RUST=ON

That option is off by default, and while it is off no Rust tool is probed and
the CMake build is exactly what it always was.  With it on, CMake performs the
same relink "make rust" does -- cargo's libz.a through zlib.map, with the same
SONAME and the same versioned symlink chain -- and then INSTALLS that set, so
"cmake --install" hands a consumer the complete drop-in and find_package(ZLIB)
resolves to it.  The two library kinds have different platform
requirements, and only one of them is ELF-only.  A SHARED Rust library needs a
linker that takes -soname and --version-script, so ZLIB_BUILD_SHARED=ON with
ZLIB_BUILD_RUST=ON is refused at configure time on Apple, AIX, SunOS and
anything not UNIX, naming the reason.  The STATIC library has no such
requirement and is supported wherever cargo runs: -DZLIB_BUILD_RUST=ON
-DZLIB_BUILD_SHARED=OFF is the portable form, and it is the row CI exercises on
macOS.  A multi-config generator and a cross-compilation toolchain are refused
at configure time in either mode, also with the reason.

Inside the Rust workspace itself, cargo drives the build directly:

    cargo build --release
    cargo build --release -p libz-rs-sys --features libz-compat

The first builds the two shipped crates with their default features, which
already include the C ABI layer.  The second names that layer explicitly, which
is the form to use when the features are being chosen by hand, and it names the
package too: this is a virtual workspace, so a bare "--features libz-compat"
leaves it to cargo to decide which selected package the feature was meant for.
Saying "-p libz-rs-sys" says it instead.

Read those as the first step of one recipe rather than as the whole of it.  The
C-ABI deliverable is the set "make rust" stages, and the three commands together
are what produce it and prove it:

    cargo build --locked --release -p libz-rs-sys --features libz-compat,gz
    make rust CARGO=cargo
    ZLIB_RS_REQUIRE_PACKAGED=1 cargo test --locked --release \
        -p libz-rs-sys --test symbol_parity -- --nocapture

The third command is the gate on the second.  Every assertion about the staged
library -- 111 symbols, 95 functions and 16 version nodes, none of zlib.map's
local names exported, the SONAME, the symlink TOPOLOGY (the versioned file real
and the two shorter names symlinks to it, not copies), and zlibCompileFlags()
bit 27 against the artifacts that ship -- inspects a file cargo does not build,
so all nine of them SKIP unless something says the library is there.
ZLIB_RS_REQUIRE_PACKAGED=1 is that something: each skip becomes a failure naming
what went unverified.  The symbols job of .github/workflows/rust.yml sets it
after staging, and additionally fails on any SKIP line, so the artifact that
ships cannot be recorded green without having been opened.

Note that "cargo build" alone does NOT produce an installable shared library,
and the shortfall is measurable: the shared object cargo emits publishes 93
dynamic global symbols where the C library publishes 111.  It carries no
zlib.map version script (rustc attaches one of its own, and a second cannot be
added), and it lacks gzprintf and gzvprintf (both variadic, so they come from the
small C shim in crates/libz-rs-sys/csrc/).  That is the whole of the gap -- two
functions and sixteen version nodes, both structural.  It does NOT export
internals: .hidden directives in crates/libz-rs-sys/src/lib.rs keep the three
zlib.map hides out of its dynamic table too, which is what took it from 96
symbols to 93.  The static libz.a cargo builds IS complete -- the shim object is
archived into it, and hidden visibility does not reach a static archive -- and
"make rust" is what relinks the shared library from
that archive under zlib.map, creates the versioned symlink chain the SONAME
libz.so.1 requires, and checks the result against zlib.h and zlib.map: every
function zlib.h declares is exported and nothing else is, the version-node count
matches zlib.map, none of zlib.map's local names is exported, and the SONAME is
the derived one.

The further comparison against the C library's own symbol table is CONDITIONAL.
"make rust" performs it only when that library exists -- ZLIBREF defaults to the
C shared library built in this tree -- and prints a note saying it was skipped
otherwise, so a green "make rust" on a tree where the C library was never built
is not a statement about parity with C.  Two commands make that comparison
mandatory instead of optional, and both fail rather than skip:

    make                                    then  make rust
    make rust-symbols ZLIBREF=/path/to/libz.so.<version>

The symbols job of .github/workflows/rust.yml takes the first route on every
push, so a parity regression fails there whether or not it was checked by
hand.

Link against what "make rust" stages, not against cargo's target/<profile>
directory.  The versioned names are deliberately absent from the latter, so a
program linked against it builds and then binds the SYSTEM libz at run time;
cargo's build script leaves a README-cargo-artifacts.txt there saying so.  Use
ldd to see which library was bound rather than inferring it from a test that
passed -- that check is exactly what "make rust-test" performs.

Installing the Rust library means installing the same names the C build
installs, with the same SONAME and the same symlink topology: the real file
libz.so.1.3.2.1-motley, with libz.so.1 and libz.so as symlinks to it, and the
static libz.a.  "make rust" stages exactly that set under target/dropin/ and
names it from ZLIB_VERSION in zlib.h, ready to be copied into place.  Read the
note above about static linking before installing the libz.a for consumers to
link through zlib.pc.

Building the Rust library needs Rust 1.80 or later and cargo -- and, for the C
ABI layer, the target's C compiler and archiver as well.  That last part is easy
to miss because the C reference implementation is emphatically NOT a build
dependency, and both things are true at once: crates/libz-rs-sys has a build
script that compiles the one translation unit its csrc/ still ships -- the
variadic gzprintf/gzvprintf pair, which stable Rust cannot declare -- and
archives it into the libz.a cargo emits.  So cc and ar (or cl.exe and lib.exe)
are ordinary prerequisites of the default facade build, exactly as they are of
the C build; "cargo build -p zlib-rs" and "cargo build -p libz-rs-sys
--no-default-features --features libz-compat" are the builds here that need
neither.  What the reference implementation's dev-only boundary buys is that it
is an ORACLE rather than a dependency: no shipped build compiles any of the
fifteen C translation units in this directory.

Two optional checksum backends are available behind the "simd" Cargo feature: a
vectorised Adler-32 and a CRC-32 that is scalar rather than vector code.  Both
change speed only, never the compressed bytes; the paragraphs after the feature
table below say what each one actually is, how much it wins, and how neutrality
is checked and where.

The workspace is edition 2021 and it has ONE floor: 1.80.  All three members --
zlib-rs, libz-rs-sys and the dev-only crates/zlib-rs-differential, which holds
the C oracle -- inherit rust-version from [workspace.package], so every command
that reaches the workspace holds to it, including "cargo test --workspace",
"cargo +1.80 check --locked --workspace --all-targets" and
"cargo +1.80 metadata --locked".  "make rust-msrv" is what checks it.

The one floor that is higher is criterion's, and it is confined to the package
that needs it.  The three benchmark suites in benches/ are their own excluded
package with their own lock file, because criterion 0.8.2 declares 1.86 and
reaches clap crates whose manifests are written in edition 2024 -- which cargo
1.80 cannot parse at all.  While those suites were attached to the differential
crate the workspace declared 1.80 and could not be resolved by 1.80, which is
not a floor so much as a claim.  So "cargo bench --manifest-path
benches/Cargo.toml" wants 1.86 and nothing else does, and the benches reach the
same oracle as before through a path dependency on the differential crate.
rust-toolchain.toml pins the stable channel, comfortably above both.

Selected Cargo features, with the crate that declares each one and its default.
This is not the whole surface -- "cargo metadata" is authoritative, and
rust/README.md carries the complete table with what each feature expands to:

    libz-compat   libz-rs-sys, on   the exported extern "C" surface
    gz            libz-rs-sys, on   the gzFile layer; it also turns on
                                    zlib-rs/std and the optional libc dependency
    libc          libz-rs-sys, off  names that optional dependency explicitly,
                                    so a downstream feature request keeps
                                    resolving; gz already enables it
    rust-api      zlib-rs, off      the idiomatic Rust surface
    simd          zlib-rs, off      a vectorised Adler-32 and a scalar
                                    stride-braid CRC-32.  libz-rs-sys
                                    and zlib-rs-differential each declare a
                                    forwarding "simd" of their own, so a
                                    package-scoped request reaches the core
                                    wherever it is spelled
    std           zlib-rs, off      the core's file I/O; otherwise no_std.  A
                                    default libz-rs-sys build turns it on through
                                    gz

The "simd" feature covers two backends and they are not the same kind of thing,
so describing them together would misdescribe one of them.  Neither contains an
architecture intrinsic -- that is what lets the safe core keep
#![forbid(unsafe_code)] -- and both are written so that LLVM has a shape it can
autovectorise at opt-level 3.  Whether it does is then a property of the
compiler and the target, and on x86_64 the answer differs between them:

  Adler-32   Vectorises.  Its body carries 91 SSE2 instructions in the release
             library -- paddd, pshufd, punpck* and their kin -- against none at
             all in the portable path it replaces.  It also carries the one
             run-time probe in either module, an is_supported() SSE2 check,
             which is a throughput hint and not a correctness gate.

  CRC-32     Does not vectorise.  Its body is 487 instructions of mov, xor, shr
             and movzbl -- zero vector instructions in the same library -- so it
             is a scalar reformulation, not a SIMD one, and it is named for what
             it is: the backend is "stride-braid", it performs no run-time
             feature test at all, and it wins by exposing instruction-level
             parallelism and by shortening the serial byte tail of the braided
             algorithm in crc32.c.  Genuine vector CRC-32 would need the
             carry-less-multiply intrinsics, which the core's safety rule rules
             out.

Both are therefore correct on a machine with no vector unit whatsoever, and what
the feature promises is the answer: both compute the same checksums as the
portable backends, on every input, so they change speed only.

The speed is measured rather than asserted, and it is enforced.  On
x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu, as candidate time over portable-backend time --
smaller is faster, and each figure is the median of nine paired rounds run in
alternating order:

    bytes        64     256      4096     65536       1 MiB
    Adler-32    0.98    0.51      0.36      0.36        0.36
    CRC-32      0.24    0.46      0.90      0.97        0.99

The sweep also covers 16, 32, 48 and 55 bytes, omitted above because at those
lengths one or both backends delegate to the portable path and the true ratio is
1.000 by construction.  The sweep does not assume that: it times the baseline
twice per round and reports a case as unmeasured, rather than giving it a ratio,
when those two identical runs disagree by more than 10%.  The module comments on
crates/zlib-rs/src/{crc32,adler32}/simd.rs carry the full nine-length tables and
say which cases that applies to.

The shapes are different because the wins have different sources.  Adler-32
gains where there is enough data to fill its lanes, so it improves as inputs
grow and merely breaks even at 64 bytes.  CRC-32 gains most between 55 and 256
bytes, where the serial epilogue it removes is most of the work, and converges
with the portable path past 4 KiB where the braided body dominates -- which is
the honest expectation for two arrangements of the same table folded over the
same words.  benches/checksum_bench.rs carries both sweeps and CI fails the
bench job if either backend is slower than the path it replaces at any measured
length, or if it is not at least 10% faster at one of them; the second condition
is there because a backend that merely forwarded to its baseline would satisfy
the first one perfectly.

Setting ZLIB_RS_SIMD=1 in the environment is how the simd feature is asked for
through make or CMake, both of which translate it into "simd" for cargo; a
direct cargo build takes "--features simd" instead, and the build script then
checks that the two requests agree rather than letting a mismatch pass
unnoticed.  The variable is an ASSERTION about the build, not a switch the build
script can honour on its own -- a build script cannot enable a Cargo feature --
and the three cases differ:

    ZLIB_RS_SIMD=1   asserts the simd feature is on.  With it off, the build
                     fails and names the feature to pass.
    ZLIB_RS_SIMD=0   asserts it is off.  With it on, the build fails and names
                     how to turn it off.
    unset            asserts nothing; the Cargo features as resolved decide.

So the default build -- no feature, no variable -- is scalar, and unset means
"no assertion" rather than "scalar": with "--features simd" and the variable
unset, the vectorised backends are built and nothing complains.
The feature reaches the two checksums and nothing else, which is what keeps it
output neutral -- a checksum is one scalar however it is computed, so a
rearranged computation cannot perturb a compressed byte.  Vectorising
match-finding would change which matches are emitted, and that is prohibited
outright.

Neutrality is checked rather than argued, and here is exactly what checks it
and on which machines.  Three things run with the feature enabled: the crate's
own equivalence sweep, which compares each optional backend against the
portable one at every path boundary and re-checks the dispatcher wiring; the
differential suites, which compare the port against the in-tree C
implementation over the committed corpus; and a 300-second cargo-fuzz run of
the checksum target that compares the two backends on fuzzer-chosen inputs.
The first two run in the all-features test pass, on all three platforms the
Rust build is gated on -- Linux, macOS and Windows.  The fuzz run is Linux
x86_64, as are the Miri and AddressSanitizer jobs.  A target outside that set
gets the same portable code and the same tests, but no measurement from us.

The gates themselves live in .github/workflows/rust.yml, and each one names
what it establishes: the differential suites against the C sources in
crates/zlib-rs-differential, Miri over the safe core with every feature enabled
(sharded by module and by test binary, since interpreting the whole crate in
one job does not finish), AddressSanitizer over the C ABI layer, the three
unmodified C drivers and the oracle harness, five cargo-fuzz targets at 300
seconds each plus the checksum target a second time with simd, the cbindgen
header compared against zlib.h -- a literal diff of all 95 contract signatures
as the C++ compiler resolves them, required to be empty, plus struct layouts and
macro values from a probe program per side, and `diff -u zlib.h
generated_zlib.h' itself published as an artifact because the two FILES
necessarily differ for the reasons cbindgen.toml enumerates -- the exported
symbols and version nodes diffed against the C library's own, and the criterion suites in benches/ measured
against the C oracle in the same process.

Questions about zlib should be sent to <zlib@gzip.org>, or to Gilles Vollant
<info@winimage.com> for the Windows DLL version.  The zlib home page is
https://zlib.net/ .  Before reporting a problem, please check this site to
verify that you have the latest version of zlib; otherwise get the latest
version and check whether the problem still exists or not.

PLEASE read the zlib FAQ https://zlib.net/zlib_faq.html before asking for help.

Mark Nelson <markn@ieee.org> wrote an article about zlib for the Jan.  1997
issue of Dr.  Dobb's Journal; a copy of the article is available at
https://zlib.net/nelson/ .

The changes made in version 1.3.2.1 are documented in the file ChangeLog.

Unsupported third party contributions are provided in directory contrib/ .

zlib is available in Java using the java.util.zip package. Follow the API
Documentation link at: https://docs.oracle.com/search/?q=java.util.zip .

A Perl interface to zlib and bzip2 written by Paul Marquess <pmqs@cpan.org>
can be found at https://github.com/pmqs/IO-Compress .

A Python interface to zlib written by A.M. Kuchling <amk@amk.ca> is
available in Python 1.5 and later versions, see
https://docs.python.org/3/library/zlib.html .

zlib is built into tcl: https://wiki.tcl-lang.org/page/zlib .

An experimental package to read and write files in .zip format, written on top
of zlib by Gilles Vollant <info@winimage.com>, is available in the
contrib/minizip directory of zlib.


Notes for some targets:

- For Windows DLL versions, please see win32/DLL_FAQ.txt

- For 64-bit Irix, deflate.c must be compiled without any optimization. With
  -O, one libpng test fails. The test works in 32 bit mode (with the -n32
  compiler flag). The compiler bug has been reported to SGI.

- zlib doesn't work with gcc 2.6.3 on a DEC 3000/300LX under OSF/1 2.1 it works
  when compiled with cc.

- On Digital Unix 4.0D (formerly OSF/1) on AlphaServer, the cc option -std1 is
  necessary to get gzprintf working correctly. This is done by configure.

- zlib doesn't work on HP-UX 9.05 with some versions of /bin/cc. It works with
  other compilers. Use "make test" to check your compiler.

- For PalmOs, see https://palmzlib.sourceforge.net/

- What the Rust workspace is VERIFIED on, exactly, rather than by tier. The CI
  matrix in .github/workflows/rust.yml builds it and runs its test suite -- the
  default features and then every feature -- on three host triples:
  x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu, aarch64-apple-darwin and x86_64-pc-windows-msvc.
  Two further targets are COMPILED and not run, to hold the integer models the
  ABI depends on: i686-unknown-linux-gnu for ILP32 and x86_64-pc-windows-gnu
  for LLP64. Everything deeper than build-and-test -- the differential
  byte-identity matrix, Miri, AddressSanitizer, the fuzz targets, the relinked
  C drivers, symbol parity, the cbindgen header check and the CMake install --
  runs on x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu alone. Other targets rustc calls tier 1 are
  expected to work and are not measured here.
- The installable SHARED drop-in is narrower still: staging it needs a linker
  that accepts -soname and --version-script, so it is offered on the ELF
  platforms that get zlib.map for the C library, and Apple, AIX, SunOS and
  anything not UNIX are refused with a message. The STATIC library carries no
  such requirement -- -DZLIB_BUILD_RUST=ON -DZLIB_BUILD_SHARED=OFF is supported
  wherever cargo runs, and CI exercises exactly that row on macOS. The platform
  makefiles in this distribution -- amiga, msdos, os400, qnx, watcom and win32
  -- build the C sources and are unaffected.


